{#
  The whole event page, rendered from whichever year is in `games`.

  Two templates include this and nothing else: index.md, where `games` is the
  current year, and years.njk, where it's each archived year in turn. Keeping
  one copy is the point — an archived year should look exactly like the front
  page did, not like a version of it that stopped being maintained.
#}
{#
  A year gets its sign-up form some time after it gets a date, so the button
  has to work before there's anywhere to send people. Until then it stays put
  and says why, rather than vanishing and changing the shape of the page.

  aria-disabled rather than the disabled attribute: a disabled button can't be
  focused, and browsers suppress mouse events on it, so neither hover nor
  keyboard focus would ever reveal the reason it's inert.

  The url is passed in rather than read from `games` inside the macro, since
  Nunjucks macros don't reliably see the enclosing template's context.
#}
{% macro registerButton(url, label) -%}
{%- if url -%}
<a class="btn btn-primary" href="{{ url }}">{{ label }}</a>
{%- else -%}
<button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" aria-disabled="true" data-tooltip="Coming soon" aria-label="{{ label }} — coming soon">{{ label }}</button>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{% set beforeCount = games.schedule.before | length %}
{% set eventCount = games.events | length %}

<section class="hero wrap">
  <div class="hero-body">
    <span class="hero-year">{{ games.startsAt | year }}</span>
    <h1 class="hero-title">
      <span class="misprint" data-text="Dad">Dad</span>
      <span class="misprint" data-text="Olympics">Olympics</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="hero-tagline">{{ games.tagline }}</p>
    <p class="hero-meta">
      <span>{{ games.startsAt | longDate }}</span><span>{{ games.startsAt | clock }}–{{ games.endsAt | clock }}</span><span>{{ games.place }}</span>
    </p>
    {{ registerButton(games.registerUrl, "Claim your bib") }}
  </div>
  <img class="hero-mascot" src="/img/barbell-mascot-680.webp"
    srcset="/img/barbell-mascot-340.webp 340w, /img/barbell-mascot-680.webp 680w"
    sizes="19rem" width="680" height="967" decoding="async"
    alt="A grinning dad pressing a barbell overhead with one arm and holding a stuffed rabbit in the other." />
</section>

{#
  The podium. Filled in after the closing ceremony — games.winners starts out
  null in the data file and the section simply doesn't render until it's a
  real list (see sectionsWithContent in lib/years.js). Sits directly under
  the hero because it's the first thing worth telling a visitor once the
  games are over, ahead of the countdown, which by then has nothing left to
  count down to.

  Ties share a place number rather than being renumbered around — two silver
  finishers are both "2nd," and there's no bronze that year. winner.tied is
  redundant with two entries sharing a place, but it's what drives the
  "Tied" label without the template having to detect duplicates itself.

  placeWords keys are quoted strings — Nunjucks dict literals reject bare
  numeric keys at compile time — and the lookup coerces winner.place to a
  string with the `string` filter to match.
#}
{% if games.sections.winners %}
{% set placeWords = {"1": "1st", "2": "2nd", "3": "3rd"} %}
<section class="section wrap" id="winners">
  <p class="eyebrow">The Results Are In</p>
  <h2>The Podium</h2>
  <ol class="podium">
    {%- for winner in games.winners %}
    <li{% if winner.medal == "gold" %} class="podium-first"{% endif %}>
      <span class="ord">{{ placeWords[winner.place | string] or winner.place }}</span>
      <span class="name">{{ winner.name }}</span>
      <span class="note">{{ winner.medal | capitalize }}{% if winner.tied %} · Tied{% endif %}</span>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ol>
</section>
{% endif %}

{#
  The countdown to the start of the games.

  It ships hidden and the script reveals it, rather than the build deciding
  whether the date is still ahead. The site is static and only rebuilds on a
  commit, so build time says nothing about when someone is actually looking at
  the page — a build-time check would leave last spring's page counting down
  to a day that has already happened. Whether the games are in the past is a
  question only the visitor's clock can answer, so the visitor's clock is the
  one place it's asked.

  Hidden by default also means visitors without JavaScript see nothing here
  instead of a frozen row of zeros.

  The digits are aria-hidden and a plain-language summary carries the same
  information for screen readers: four numbers changing every second is noise
  read aloud, and no live region is used so it isn't re-announced on tick.
#}
<section class="countdown wrap" data-countdown="{{ games.startsAt }}" aria-label="Time remaining until the games" hidden>
  <h2 class="countdown-title">The Countdown</h2>
  <p class="countdown-subtitle">Until the torch is lit</p>
  <ol class="countdown-clock" aria-hidden="true">
    <li><span class="countdown-num" data-unit="days">00</span><span class="countdown-label">Days</span></li>
    <li><span class="countdown-num" data-unit="hours">00</span><span class="countdown-label">Hours</span></li>
    <li><span class="countdown-num" data-unit="minutes">00</span><span class="countdown-label">Minutes</span></li>
    <li><span class="countdown-num" data-unit="seconds">00</span><span class="countdown-label">Seconds</span></li>
  </ol>
  <p class="countdown-summary" data-countdown-summary></p>
</section>

{#
  The invitation disappears once the games have started — "you are cordially
  invited" stops making sense to read after the fact. Keyed off the same
  games.startsAt timestamp as the countdown above, and decided the same way:
  by the visitor's clock, via data-hide-after, since a static build has no
  way to know whether "now" is before or after that moment for whoever is
  looking at the page. See src/js/countdown.js.

  Unlike the countdown, this section ships visible. There's no equivalent of
  "freeze at zero" to avoid here, so the JS-off fallback is just to leave it
  showing — stale copy is harmless where a frozen countdown wasn't.
#}
{% if games.sections.invitation %}
<section class="section wrap" data-hide-after="{{ games.startsAt }}">
  <p class="eyebrow">The Invitation</p>
  <h2>You are cordially invited</h2>
  {%- if games.invitation.lede %}
  <p class="lede">{{ games.invitation.lede }}</p>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- for paragraph in games.invitation.paragraphs %}
  <p>{{ paragraph }}</p>
  {%- endfor %}
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if games.sections.events %}
<section class="section wrap" id="events">
  <p class="eyebrow">{{ eventCount | numberWord }} Official Events</p>
  <h2>The Games</h2>
  <ol class="events">
    {%- for event in games.events %}
    <li class="event">
      <span class="event-num">{{ loop.index | pad2 }}</span>
      <h3>{{ event.name }}</h3>
      <p>{{ event.description }}</p>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ol>
  {%- for event in games.events %}
  {%- if event.notice %}
  <div class="notice">
    <p><strong>{{ event.notice.title }}</strong> {{ event.notice.body }}</p>
  </div>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- endfor %}
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if games.sections.schedule %}
<section class="section wrap" id="schedule">
  <p class="eyebrow">About Two Hours, Start to Finish</p>
  <h2>Order of the Day</h2>
  <ol class="schedule">
    {%- for item in games.schedule.before %}
    <li>
      <span class="ord">{{ loop.index }}</span>
      <span class="name">{{ item.name }}</span>
      <span class="note">{{ item.note }}</span>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
    {%- for event in games.events %}
    <li>
      <span class="ord">{{ loop.index + beforeCount }}</span>
      <span class="name">{{ event.name }}</span>
      <span class="note">{{ event.summary }}</span>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
    {%- for item in games.schedule.after %}
    <li>
      <span class="ord">{{ loop.index + beforeCount + eventCount }}</span>
      <span class="name">{{ item.name }}</span>
      <span class="note">{{ item.note }}</span>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ol>
  <p class="fine">
    There will be 10–15 minutes between events while points are tallied, cones are moved, mothers
    are consulted, and somebody's kid is located.
  </p>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if games.sections.whatToBring %}
<section class="section wrap" id="what-to-bring">
  <p class="eyebrow">Come Prepared</p>
  <h2>What to Bring</h2>
  {%- if games.whatToBring.items | length %}
  <ul class="checklist">
    {%- for item in games.whatToBring.items %}
    <li>{{ item }}</li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ul>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- if games.whatToBring.notice %}
  <div class="notice">
    <p><strong>{{ games.whatToBring.notice.title }}</strong> {{ games.whatToBring.notice.body }}</p>
  </div>
  {%- endif %}
</section>
{% endif %}

<section class="section wrap">
  <div class="rule-quote">
    <blockquote>No hardos.</blockquote>
    <cite>The Committee &middot; Rule One</cite>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cta wrap">
  <h2>See you on the lawn</h2>
  <p>
    Fill out the form so we know you're coming. More details to follow as the day gets closer.
  </p>
  <p>{{ registerButton(games.registerUrl, "Register your family") }}</p>
</section>

<div class="mascot-band">
  <img src="/img/barbell-mascot-340.webp"
    srcset="/img/barbell-mascot-340.webp 340w, /img/barbell-mascot-680.webp 680w"
    sizes="13rem" width="680" height="967" decoding="async" loading="lazy"
    alt="A grinning dad pressing a barbell overhead with one arm and holding a stuffed rabbit in the other." />
</div>
